<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>

    <!--
        Login, signup and reset-password.

        One inherit covers all three: web.login, auth_signup.signup and
        auth_signup.reset_password every one of them t-calls
        web.login_layout, so this is the single place where the card is
        built. That is also why the manifest does not depend on
        auth_signup — the template being extended lives in web, and the
        signup pages pick the styling up if and only if that module
        happens to be installed.

        ── Why this replaces the layout wholesale ──────────────────

        It used to be four small xpaths into core's markup (a t-set on
        body_classname, two blocks before the card, and the logo). That
        broke the moment `website` was installed, and silently:

            website/views/website_templates.xml
              <template id="login_layout" inherit_id="web.login_layout"
                        priority="20">
                <xpath expr="t[@t-call]" position="replace">
                  <t t-call="website.layout">
                    <div class="oe_website_login_container" t-out="0"/>
                  </t>
                </xpath>

        That replace takes out the *entire* `t-call` subtree — core's
        card, core's logo and everything this module had just added
        inside it — and hands the bare form to website.layout instead.
        With the o_bluenova_auth_page class gone from <body>, every rule
        in scss/auth_pages.scss is scoped to a class that is no longer
        on the page, so the login screen falls back to whatever palette
        the website theme happens to be using. No error, no warning; the
        page simply stops being themed.

        So this view does the same thing website's does — replace the
        one `t-call` child — at priority 30, which is *after* website's
        20. Both structures end at the same place, whether website is
        installed or not, and the login screen looks the same either
        way.

        Two notes on the replacement body:

        • It t-calls web.frontend_layout, not website.layout, and that
          is correct even with website installed. website.layout is a
          non-primary inherit of portal.frontend_layout, which is a
          non-primary inherit of web.frontend_layout — all three names
          resolve to one combined arch. Calling the base name renders
          the website-aware layout when website is there and the plain
          one when it is not, without this module ever naming a
          template it does not depend on.

        • no_header / no_footer stay True, so the website's own header
          and footer do not appear on the auth pages. That is the point
          of the fix: these three screens are the theme's, and they look
          identical on an instance with website and one without. An
          admin who would rather keep the website chrome around the
          login card only has to drop those two t-sets.

        Everything outside the branding block below is core's own markup
        from web.login_layout, kept verbatim — the card classes the
        database manager keys on (o_database_list), the
        login_card_classes hook, and the footer with its
        disable_footer / disable_database_manager guards.
    -->
    <template id="auth_theme_login_layout" inherit_id="web.login_layout"
              name="BlueNova Themed Auth Pages" priority="30">

        <!--
            Relative, not `//t[@t-call]`: it has to match the single
            direct child of the template root in both shapes — core's
            `t-call="web.frontend_layout"` and, when website's inherit
            has already run, its `t-call="website.layout"`. This is the
            same expression website itself uses.
        -->
        <xpath expr="t[@t-call]" position="replace">
            <t t-call="web.frontend_layout">
                <t t-set="html_data" t-value="{'style': 'height: 100%;'}"/>

                <!--
                    The scope class.

                    Everything auth_pages.scss styles hangs off this one
                    class, so the stylesheet can never leak into the
                    backend even though both share the same token names.
                    It goes on <body> — via core's own body_classname
                    hook rather than a wrapper div — which is what lets
                    the page background be styled too.
                -->
                <t t-set="body_classname" t-value="'bg-100 o_bluenova_auth_page'"/>
                <t t-set="no_header" t-value="True"/>
                <t t-set="no_footer" t-value="True"/>

                <!--
                    The colours saved under Settings > Theme Settings.

                    The same _get_theme_css() the backend head uses (see
                    views/theme_styles.xml), rendered here because the
                    login page never loads web.webclient_bootstrap.
                    request.env is available on this unauthenticated
                    request: ensure_db() has already run by the time the
                    controller renders, and web.frontend_layout — the
                    template this one is nested in — calls request.env
                    itself for the session info.

                    First in the body, so it beats the compiled bundles
                    on document order: they are in <head>.
                -->
                <t t-set="cmt_settings" t-value="request.env['res.config.settings'].sudo()"/>
                <t t-set="cmt_theme_css" t-value="cmt_settings._get_theme_css()"/>
                <style t-if="cmt_theme_css" type="text/css" t-out="cmt_theme_css"/>

                <!--
                    And the dark block. Its selector is the exact
                    opposite of the light one, so only ever one of the
                    two matches; the script below decides which by
                    setting the attribute.
                -->
                <t t-set="cmt_theme_css_dark" t-value="cmt_settings._get_theme_css_dark()"/>
                <style t-if="cmt_theme_css_dark" type="text/css" t-out="cmt_theme_css_dark"/>

                <!--
                    Dark mode, before the first paint.

                    static/src/js/theme_mode.js cannot do this job here:
                    it is in web.assets_backend, which the login page
                    never loads, and the frontend bundle is deferred
                    anyway — a module lands after the page has already
                    been painted light and the card would visibly flip.
                    This reads the same localStorage key that file
                    writes ('cmt_color_scheme') and sets the same
                    attribute auth_pages.scss keys on. Kept inline and
                    kept tiny for that reason.
                -->
                <script type="text/javascript">
                    try {
                        if (window.localStorage.getItem('cmt_color_scheme') === 'dark') {
                            document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-cmt-theme', 'dark');
                        }
                    } catch (e) {
                        /* private browsing: no stored preference, stay light */
                    }
                </script>

                <div class="container py-5">
                    <!--
                        Core's card, verbatim — including the inline
                        max-width, which auth_pages.scss overrides with
                        !important rather than removing here, so a
                        stale or missing frontend bundle still renders
                        the stock 300px card instead of a full-width
                        one.
                    -->
                    <div t-attf-class="card border-0 mx-auto bg-100 {{login_card_classes}} o_database_list" style="max-width: 300px;">
                        <div class="card-body">
                            <div class="text-center pb-3 border-bottom mb-4">
                                <!--
                                    Branding.

                                    The uploaded sidebar brand image
                                    doubles as the login logo, so one
                                    upload dresses both surfaces.
                                    _get_auth_branding() returns
                                    logo_url = False when nothing is
                                    uploaded or the admin has turned the
                                    image off, and the original company
                                    logo is kept in that case — an
                                    untouched install still shows
                                    exactly what it showed before.
                                -->
                                <t t-set="cmt_branding" t-value="cmt_settings._get_auth_branding()"/>
                                <img t-if="cmt_branding['logo_url']" class="o_bluenova_auth_logo"
                                     t-att-src="cmt_branding['logo_url']" alt="Logo"/>
                                <img t-else="" class="o_bluenova_auth_logo"
                                     t-attf-src="/web/binary/company_logo{{ '?dbname='+db if db else '' }}"
                                     alt="Logo" style="max-height:120px; max-width: 100%; width:auto"/>

                                <!--
                                    The heading, per the reference
                                    design.

                                    All three pages share this one
                                    template, so which heading to show
                                    has to come from the request path —
                                    web.login_layout has no idea which
                                    of its three callers is rendering
                                    it, and the two signup templates
                                    live in auth_signup, a module this
                                    one deliberately does not depend on.

                                    Falls back to nothing rather than to
                                    "Welcome back" on an unrecognised
                                    path: a heading is better absent
                                    than wrong, and another module could
                                    route a fourth page through this
                                    same layout.
                                -->
                                <t t-set="cmt_path" t-value="request.httprequest.path or ''"/>
                                <t t-if="'reset_password' in cmt_path">
                                    <h1 class="o_bluenova_auth_heading">Reset Password</h1>
                                    <p class="o_bluenova_auth_lede">Please enter your details to reset your password</p>
                                </t>
                                <t t-elif="'signup' in cmt_path">
                                    <h1 class="o_bluenova_auth_heading">Create your account</h1>
                                    <p class="o_bluenova_auth_lede">Please enter your details to get started</p>
                                </t>
                                <t t-elif="'login' in cmt_path">
                                    <h1 class="o_bluenova_auth_heading">Welcome back</h1>
                                    <p class="o_bluenova_auth_lede">Please enter your details to sign in</p>
                                </t>

                                <!--
                                    t-out on a plain string, never
                                    Markup: the tagline is admin-entered
                                    free text and lands on a page served
                                    to anonymous visitors, so it is
                                    escaped.
                                -->
                                <div t-if="cmt_branding['tagline']" class="o_bluenova_auth_tagline">
                                    <t t-out="cmt_branding['tagline']"/>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <!-- The form itself: web.login, auth_signup.signup or
                                 auth_signup.reset_password, whichever called us. -->
                            <t t-out="0"/>

                            <div class="text-center small mt-4 pt-3 border-top" t-if="not disable_footer">
                                <t t-if="not disable_database_manager">
                                    <a class="border-end pe-2 me-1" href="/web/database/manager">Manage Databases</a>
                                </t>
                                <a href="https://www.odoo.com?utm_source=db&amp;utm_medium=auth" target="_blank">Powered by <span>Odoo</span></a>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </t>
        </xpath>

    </template>

</odoo>
